Promise :
=========
→ A Promise is like a placeholder for a future value — the result is not available immediately, but it 
will be available at some point.
→Promise object called as placeholder which will be filled later with a value 
→promise object is a placeholder for until certain period of time until we receive value from 
asynchronous operations 
→Promises in Node.js are used to handle asynchronous operations more cleanly compared to callbacks.
→ Two important issues we face that remain above….
→ Promises help manage tasks like file reading, API calls, or database queries without blocking the main
 thread. 
→A Promise has three states: pending, fulfilled, and rejected. Pending means the operation is still 
running. Fulfilled means it succeeded and returned a result. Rejected means it failed and returned an 
error. 
→We use .then() to handle success and .catch() to handle failure.”** 

→Earlier, we used callbacks for these async tasks, but callbacks create many problems. When we put 
callbacks inside callbacks — like createOrder → proceedToPayment → sendSMS — the code becomes nested and 
messy. This is called callback hell. It’s hard to read, hard to debug, and if the flow grows, it becomes 
unmanageable, grows horizontally.”
→ “Another big issue is Inversion of Control. When we pass our callback into another function like 
createOrder, we give control to that function. We are trusting createOrder to call our function correctly. But if createOrder has bugs or calls our callback twice, then proceedToPayment will also run twice, meaning payment can be deducted twice. So we lose control over our own logic
→We given control of payment to createOrder API so it is the responsibility of createOrder API to 
call our function back, and we are blindly trusting createOrder api and there is lot of bugs inside 
create order api and inter-communication problem that we face when we are using call back Promises were 
    introduced to solve these problems.
Promises give us a guarantee that a function will resolve only once or reject only once.
 So even if createOrder tries to resolve twice, JavaScript ignores the second call.
 This solves the inversion of control problem because our next step runs only once.”
“Promises also solve callback hell.
 Instead of nesting callbacks, we can chain .then() and .catch() in a clean flat structure.”

function createOrder(cart) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    resolve("ORDER123");      // ✔ first resolve works
    resolve("ORDER456");      // ❌ ignored
    reject("Some error");     // ❌ ignored
  });
}


